1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,840
Here's a pretty normal DeFi request:

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:07,113
stake ETH with Lido and borrow USDC against it on Aave.

3
00:00:07,153 --> 00:00:11,113
And the same on Solana, with Marinade and Kamino.

4
00:00:13,163 --> 00:00:16,072
On the left is Claude Code with no harness.

5
00:00:16,112 --> 00:00:19,776
It has to go find the contract addresses, fetch the ABIs,

6
00:00:19,816 --> 00:00:23,293
ask you for an RPC endpoint, and write its own script.

7
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:25,871
Then it hits a revert, error 27,

8
00:00:25,911 --> 00:00:31,153
because Aave actually takes wstETH, not stETH.

9
00:00:31,193 --> 00:00:35,379
And then it asks for your private key in a .env file.

10
00:00:35,419 --> 00:00:38,433
On the right it's the same model, through Aomi.

11
00:00:38,473 --> 00:00:41,022
It reads the wrap rate, simulates the stake,

12
00:00:41,062 --> 00:00:42,288
checks the loan-to-value,

13
00:00:42,328 --> 00:00:46,532
and all six steps pass on a fork as one bundle.

14
00:00:47,082 --> 00:00:50,258
On Solana it's the same story with different failures:

15
00:00:50,298 --> 00:00:52,011
a rate-limited RPC,

16
00:00:52,051 --> 00:00:54,786
a missing token account, Kamino's refresh rule,

17
00:00:54,826 --> 00:00:56,876
and the transaction size limit.

18
00:00:56,916 --> 00:00:59,023
Aomi simulates it all in Lite

19
00:00:59,063 --> 00:01:02,850
S V M and sends one transaction.

20
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:05,254
The point of having a harness is performance,

21
00:01:05,294 --> 00:01:07,541
token efficiency, and security.

22
00:01:07,581 --> 00:01:10,107
A lot of people argue frontier models

23
00:01:10,147 --> 00:01:12,139
can transact without any harness,

24
00:01:12,179 --> 00:01:13,300
and they kind of can.

25
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:16,202
But a harness makes them work better,

26
00:01:16,242 --> 00:01:17,839
so if you want to productionize

27
00:01:17,879 --> 00:01:19,651
an agentic financial workflow,

28
00:01:19,691 --> 00:01:21,004
it's a no-brainer.

29
00:01:21,055 --> 00:01:23,337
The harness is the loop around the model:

30
00:01:23,377 --> 00:01:25,578
it gets the context, builds the call,

31
00:01:25,618 --> 00:01:28,457
simulates it, then commits, and if anything fails,

32
00:01:28,497 --> 00:01:31,157
it goes back and reads again.

33
00:01:31,607 --> 00:01:34,179
First, context instantiation.

34
00:01:34,219 --> 00:01:37,233
Reading chain state and wallet state is deterministic,

35
00:01:37,273 --> 00:01:38,893
so it doesn't need intelligence.

36
00:01:38,933 --> 00:01:41,656
It can basically be shrunk to a software function,

37
00:01:41,696 --> 00:01:44,907
so what took Claude a dozen

38
00:01:44,947 --> 00:01:47,468
calls is one call for the model.

39
00:01:47,718 --> 00:01:49,791
Then transaction construction.

40
00:01:49,831 --> 00:01:51,962
The model just says what to call.

41
00:01:52,002 --> 00:01:53,576
The harness builds the bytes,

42
00:01:53,616 --> 00:01:56,525
and its protocol skills already know the rules,

43
00:01:56,565 --> 00:01:59,636
like Aave wanting wstETH,

44
00:01:59,676 --> 00:02:02,469
or Kamino needing a reserve refresh.

45
00:02:02,509 --> 00:02:05,576
Those are the things Claude had to learn by failing.

46
00:02:09,026 --> 00:02:11,006
Those same failures still happen here,

47
00:02:11,046 --> 00:02:14,717
but they happen on a fork, before anything is sent.

48
00:02:16,367 --> 00:02:19,566
Every call runs against a fork of live state first.

49
00:02:19,606 --> 00:02:20,414
When it reverts,

50
00:02:20,454 --> 00:02:23,258
the error comes back to the model as back pressure,

51
00:02:23,298 --> 00:02:25,870
and only a green run gets committed.

52
00:02:25,910 --> 00:02:29,028
It's the same idea as CI for coding agents:

53
00:02:29,068 --> 00:02:31,176
tests push back when something breaks,

54
00:02:31,216 --> 00:02:33,574
so the model writes good code.

55
00:02:38,225 --> 00:02:39,543
Once everything passes,

56
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:42,899
Aomi compresses the sequence into one transaction:

57
00:02:42,939 --> 00:02:45,348
a smart wallet bundle on Ethereum,

58
00:02:45,388 --> 00:02:48,112
one versioned transaction on Solana.

59
00:02:48,152 --> 00:02:50,085
That saves gas and settlement time,

60
00:02:50,125 --> 00:02:52,043
and the model does less.

61
00:02:52,993 --> 00:02:55,240
And the key never goes to the model.

62
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:58,549
Commit hands your wallet a signable transaction,

63
00:02:58,589 --> 00:02:59,629
and you sign it,

64
00:02:59,669 --> 00:03:02,508
or a scoped session or an agent wallet

65
00:03:02,548 --> 00:03:04,907
signs within the limits you set.

66
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:10,014
On Solana, a Swig role enforces those limits onchain.

67
00:03:12,265 --> 00:03:15,406
Then the chain settles it like any other transaction,

68
00:03:15,446 --> 00:03:17,426
and Aomi checks the result against

69
00:03:17,466 --> 00:03:19,906
the simulation you approved.

70
00:03:22,756 --> 00:03:25,282
Performance, token efficiency, and security.

71
00:03:25,322 --> 00:03:27,146
That's what the harness adds.

72
00:03:27,796 --> 00:03:29,672
The model interprets the intent,

73
00:03:29,712 --> 00:03:31,332
your wallet holds the key,

74
00:03:31,372 --> 00:03:34,833
and Aomi facilitates everything in between.
